# @boundary warns too few arguments to 'add': expected 2, found 1
# @boundary-pending BUG-142  too-few arguments warns instead of erroring
#
# BOUNDARY: FEWER arguments than the function declares, with no default to cover
# the gap. Written in STATEMENT position deliberately — this is the control that
# proves arity checking exists at all, which is what makes bv_arity_interp_
# unchecked.zbr's silence attributable to the interpolation context.
#
# INTENT vs TODAY, recorded rather than legislated: this probe was first written to
# assert a hard REJECTION. The compiler warns and compiles, padding the missing
# argument with a deterministic zero. That is not an oversight — BUG-142 chose it
# deliberately, and its own text names "promoting the warning to a hard error" as
# the remaining work. A boundary suite does not get to overturn a deliberate
# decision by asserting its own opinion, so this pins the warning and carries the
# ticket. If the promotion happens, this probe fails and becomes `rejects`.
def add(a: int, b: int): int
    return a + b

def main()
    var r = add(1)
    print(r)
